  rights: [R('rm-d026', 'A lawful notice closes a shelter in a cold wave with no alternative. What does the right to life require of the authority?', [
    ['a', 'Not to close without an alternative: shelter in a cold wave is part of the right to life, so the order must be amended or suspended.', true, 'Correct. A lawful power must still be exercised consistently with the right to life and dignity.'],
    ['b', 'To enforce the notice; it is lawful, and the weather is not the authority’s problem.', false, 'Lawful powers cannot be used to expose people to life-threatening cold with no alternative.'],
    ['c', 'To move the residents out of the city so the shelter can close.', false, 'Citizens cannot be removed from their own city; out of sight is not a lawful remedy.'],
  ], 'The right to life and dignity (Article 21) includes shelter in life-threatening conditions; a lawful closure power must be exercised with a real alternative in place.', 'A hall unlocked till half past seven and an amended order kept ninety people alive and the renovation on schedule.', SRC.liberty, {
    question: 'A legal notice closes a shelter in a cold wave with no other place to go. What must the authority do?',
    options: ['Not close it without a warm alternative. Change or pause the order.', 'Enforce it. The weather is not their problem.', 'Move the residents out of the city.'],
    feedback: ['Yes! A legal power still has to protect life.', 'No. A rule cannot put people in deadly cold with nowhere to go.', 'People cannot be removed from their own city.'],
    principle: 'Shelter in deadly cold is part of the right to life. A closure needs a real alternative first.',
    why: 'An unlocked hall and a fixed order kept ninety people alive and the repairs on schedule.',
  })],
